}}

==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Lunos the Eclipsed''' at level 10.

==Strategy/Usage==
Lunos the Eclipsed completely breaks the idea of cash management in half. The base unit himself is an anti-Traitless/Floating/Alien/Zombie tank with very decent overall stats. Against targets, he excels with 459,000 effective HP vs. [[Fruit Treasures|Fruited traits]], and 367,200 HP vs. Traitless (729,000/583,000 at Lv.50). This gives him fine enough staying power, if not making him a bit flimsy against non-targets as [[Resistant|Resistance]] is why that heart still beats. Lunos usually relies on [[Knockback]] to pick up the slack and push a majority of threats away from him, especially since his damage tends to be lacking. His immunities also prevent enemies with gimmicky abilities like [[Star Peng]], [[Gobble]], and even [[Zollow]] from repositioning him so easily. Truthfully, there's not a lot in his kit that makes Lunos the Eclipsed such a disgustingly broken unit.

His [[Conjure]] is where the real crux of his power lies. It possesses the [[Extra Money]] ability and creates a Lv.6 [[Wave Attack]]. Given the sheer damage of the summon (72,900 at Lv.50, which is the same as a [[Talents|Talented]] [[Hip Hop Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Can Can Cat]] at equal levels ''without'' factoring in its Wave Attack), and the fact that it can snipe enemies up to 1,333 range away, it has the potential to single-handedly fill up your wallet and clear hordes of peons all within a single cast. Many of the more noteworthy small-fry enemies with high cash drops like [[Doge Dark]], [[Gabriel]], [[Sir Seal]], [[Owlbrow]], [[Pigeon de Sable]], [[Pigge]], [[Mistress Celeboodle]], etc., are all instantly killed by it at standard [[Uncanny Legends]] magnification (depending on his level). Even if it fails to kill anything remarkable, the conjure heavily cripples everything caught in its crossfire, giving other units the opportunity to get the job done themselves. The Wave Attack can also hit enemies knocked back by the summon's initial attack, given they're far enough away. All of this is compounded by his 10-second recharge rate, letting you get another Lunos out on the field at the snap of a finger if he ever meets his premature demise. Given how efficiently he pays himself off, it is actually very easy to brute-force a large majority of stages by simply repeatedly cycling him to maximize the sheer damage and utility of his Conjure. In the right circumstances, it can even supply you with an unending lifeline of cash.

On that note, Lunos the Eclipsed is versatile not only by himself, but also in tandem with other expensive units as a sort of battery. He can be used as a catalyst for expensive strategies that are otherwise unsustainable thanks to his phenomenal offensive and defensive support, alongside the insane income provided by his Conjure. To say Lunos the Eclipsed is busted would be severely underselling him, as he is amazing even by ordinary EPICFEST standards.

